.TH WREX-NONEXT 7 "2026-10-18" "wrex 0.1.0" "wrex Manual"
.SH NAME
wrex-nonext \- nonext layer for \fBwrex\fR(1)

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBwrex\fR \fIcraft\fR|\fIsend\fR [options] \fB--layer\fR \fInonext\fR \fB--nonext\-<field>\fR=\fIVALUE\fR ...
\fBwrex\fR \fIfields\fR \fInonext\fR

.SH DESCRIPTION
IPv6 no\-next\-header sentinel (RFC 8200 §4.6, next\-header 59): a zero\-extent terminal marker layer — it consumes no bytes and carries no fields, but materializes as a real layer so filters can speak `nonext` presence and craft can terminate a stack (`\-\-layer ipv6 \-\-layer nonext`; the ipv6 next_header autofills 59). Zero\-length spans are safe through the reassemble/coverage invariants (verified by the tranche\-17a units).
.

.SH ENCAPSULATION
Rank 25; stacks on ipv6 (parents).

.SH "FIELD FLAGS"
Dots in field paths become dashes in flags (ipv4.ttl -> --ipv4-ttl, --nonext-<field>).
Explicit values always win over autofill and are kept
unvalidated; \fB\-\-strict\fR re-validates (violations exit 2).

.SH EXAMPLES
wrex fields nonext

